WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.929
A card payment is declined ten percent of the time, independently

00:04.029 --> 00:07.710
of the others. A customer makes four payments today. How many

00:07.810 --> 00:11.367
of them get declined? Zero is likely, one is possible, four

00:11.467 --> 00:15.024
would be a very bad day. Let's find the whole distribution.

00:17.116 --> 00:20.010
The same pattern appears whenever we count successes

00:20.110 --> 00:22.946
in a fixed number of independent, identical trials.

00:24.033 --> 00:27.264
Each payment either goes through or is declined, so four

00:27.364 --> 00:31.190
payments give two to the fourth, sixteen possible sequences. Let's

00:31.290 --> 00:33.807
sort them by how many declines they contain.

00:33.900 --> 00:38.088
There is one sequence with no declines, four with exactly

00:38.188 --> 00:41.700
one, six with two, four with three, and one with

00:41.800 --> 00:45.989
all four declined. Those counts are just 'four choose y':

00:46.089 --> 00:49.525
the number of ways to pick which payments fail.

00:50.616 --> 00:54.247
Now the probability of any one sequence. Take fail, succeed,

00:54.347 --> 00:58.476
succeed, fail. By independence, multiply: point one times point nine

00:58.576 --> 01:02.021
times point nine times point one. Every sequence with two

01:02.121 --> 01:05.876
declines has exactly this probability; only the order differs.

01:06.466 --> 01:11.528
So the probability of y declines is the number of such sequences, times

01:11.628 --> 01:16.980
the probability of each one. That's the whole binomial formula: n choose y,

01:17.080 --> 01:20.107
times p to the y, times q to the n minus y.

01:21.200 --> 01:25.055
Now take twenty payments and watch the shape as p changes. With

01:25.155 --> 01:28.885
a small p the distribution huddles near zero and is skewed to

01:28.985 --> 01:32.652
the right. As p grows, the hump moves right and becomes more

01:32.752 --> 01:35.728
symmetric. The balance point is always n times p.

01:35.816 --> 01:38.500
And the spread is n p q. It's largest at

01:38.600 --> 01:41.702
p equals one half, when each trial is the most

01:41.802 --> 01:45.460
uncertain, and it shrinks as p approaches zero or one.

01:46.550 --> 01:49.372
So when you count successes in a fixed number of

01:49.472 --> 01:53.268
independent trials with the same chance, the binomial formula is

01:53.368 --> 01:57.103
just counting times multiplying: the number of sequences with y

01:57.203 --> 02:00.268
successes, times the probability of any one of them.
